    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the suggestion!\r\n\r\nThe explicit constraints in configuration was an intentional choice, so allowing variable chroma level is out of question. If you want to precisely control the palette, there are [`make_palette()`](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/5c72e4d74e05f50325641e5fa921fca97a24cb13/doc/mini-hues.txt#L226) (to make/get the current palette), [`MiniColors.convert()`](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/5c72e4d74e05f50325641e5fa921fca97a24cb13/doc/mini-colors.txt#L785) (to convert between Hex and Oklch color spaces), and [`apply_palette()`](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/5c72e4d74e05f50325641e5fa921fca97a24cb13/doc/mini-hues.txt#L307) (to apply the palette).\r\n\r\nI was going to close this, but decided to take a look at which chroma levels are low-medium-high. Those are 4-8-16. So maybe fitting a 12 there is reasonable. Unfortunately, I don't have a good name for something between \"medium\" and \"high\", so will have to think if it is worth making a breaking change and make \"high\" be chroma 12 and something new (like \"max\") be 16. If there are name suggestions for something between \"medium\" and \"high\", that would be helpful.\r\n\r\n**Edit**: maybe \"moderate\", \"rich\", and \"vivid\" are good values. For once LLMs are useful :)",
